// NOOR Guide, curated Q&A for the "Ask about this chapter" clarifier panel.
// Keys are "n:<nodeId>" or "c:<charId>". Plain script, no modules.
const NOOR_GUIDE = {
  topics: {

    "n:1": {
      intro: "If the beginning of all things stirs a question in you, ask it gently here.",
      qa: [
        { q: "Why did Allah create the universe at all?",
          a: "Not from need: Allah is free of need of the worlds (29:6), and He tells us He created jinn and mankind to worship Him (51:56), to know and love the One whose mercy comes first. Above His Throne He wrote: My mercy prevails over My wrath (Bukhari 3194). So creation opens inside mercy, not inside lack, and every chapter that follows moves within that sentence." },
        { q: "If everything was written 50,000 years before creation, do my choices still matter?",
          a: "The Pen recorded what Allah already knows (Muslim 2653), and knowing a choice is not forcing it: you meet your decisions as real because they are. When the Companions asked why they should strive at all, the Prophet ﷺ answered: work, for each is eased toward what he was created for (Muslim 2647). The believer holds both truths at once: nothing that reaches you could have missed you, and nothing you were commanded is out of your hands." },
        { q: "What does Kun fayakun actually mean?",
          a: "When Allah intends a thing, He only says to it: Be, and it is (36:82). It is decree rather than effort: no tools, no delay, no resistance. This chapter calls it the first lesson of the whole Path: the One who begins the story can carry it wherever He wills." }
      ]
    },

    "n:2": {
      intro: "The refusal of Iblis and the slip of Adam raise honest questions; bring them here.",
      qa: [
        { q: "Why did Allah allow Iblis to refuse, and then let him tempt mankind?",
          a: "Allah created beings who truly choose, and a command that cannot be refused would leave no meaning in obedience; Iblis chose pride, refusing out of self-regard while knowing the command (2:34). His reach was then strictly limited: he can invite and whisper, but he has no authority to compel, as this chapter says plainly. The angels asked a similar question about mankind and were answered: I know what you do not know (2:30); the rest of the Path is that answer unfolding." },
        { q: "Did Adam's slip place mankind under inherited sin?",
          a: "No. Both Adam and Hawwa ate, both repented, and Allah accepted their repentance in full (2:37); no child carries the guilt of it. The descent to earth was not a hopeless punishment but the opening of the trust, for: I am placing on earth a khalifah (2:30) was announced before the slip. Repentance, not inherited guilt, is the first human model." },
        { q: "Why did the angels prostrate to Adam if worship belongs to Allah alone?",
          a: "The prostration was honour and obedience to Allah's command, not worship of Adam; worship belongs to Allah alone, as this chapter is careful to say. Adam had just been taught the names when the angels could not produce them, and the prostration marked the station Allah chose for him. Iblis's refusal of that same command is what condemned him: knowledge of the command joined to refusal from pride." }
      ]
    },

    "n:4": {
      intro: "The first blood in human history is a hard page; ask what weighs on you.",
      qa: [
        { q: "Why did Allah accept one brother's offering and refuse the other's?",
          a: "The Qur'an gives the reason in one line: Allah only accepts from the God-conscious (5:27). Acceptance follows taqwa, not lineage, gift, or display. Qabil's tragedy was answering that verdict by attacking his brother instead of correcting his own heart." },
        { q: "Why would Allah let the very first death be a murder?",
          a: "The earth was made a place of trial, and the first crime shows what envy, the disease of Iblis, can do even inside one household. From that grave the Qur'an legislated the sanctity of every life until the end of time: whoever kills one soul unjustly, it is as if he had slain mankind entirely (5:32). And the Prophet ﷺ warned that a share of every unjust killing returns to the first son of Adam, who established it (Bukhari 3335): evil begun is evil answered for." },
        { q: "Habil let himself be killed. Is that weakness?",
          a: "This chapter calls it restraint, not weakness: if you stretch out your hand to kill me, I will not stretch out mine, I fear Allah, Lord of the worlds (5:28). He preferred meeting Allah wronged over meeting Him as a wrongdoer. Islam permits defending oneself; what Habil models is the refusal to race a brother into sin." }
      ]
    },

    "n:9": {
      intro: "If the fire that cooled invites wonder or doubt, ask freely.",
      qa: [
        { q: "Did the fire really become cool, or is that a metaphor?",
          a: "The Qur'an records a command addressed to the fire itself: O fire, be coolness and peace upon Ibrahim (21:69), and the tradition reads it as an event, not an image. Fire burns only by its Owner's permission, so its Owner may suspend it. The chapter keeps the small witness the Prophet ﷺ preserved: even the gecko took a side that day, blowing on the blaze (Bukhari 3359)." },
        { q: "Why doesn't Allah rescue every believer the way He rescued Ibrahim?",
          a: "Miracles of that kind are signs sealed to prophethood, not a general exemption from trial; the same Ibrahim was still tested with exile and the sacrifice. What is offered to everyone is what he said as he was thrown: Allah is sufficient for us, and the best Disposer of affairs (Bukhari 4563), and the certainty that no trial falls outside Allah's hand. Sometimes the believer is saved from the fire, and sometimes honoured through it; both endings are held in His mercy." },
        { q: "Do miracles mean the laws of nature are not real?",
          a: "In Islamic teaching, what we call laws are Allah's customary way with His creation, kept so faithfully that we can build our lives on it. A miracle is not chaos but signature: the Owner of the habit setting it aside briefly to authenticate His messenger. Ibrahim himself argued from the habit's reliability: Allah brings the sun from the east, so bring it from the west (2:258)." }
      ]
    },

    "n:14": {
      intro: "Yusuf's long road from the well to the throne carries a question many hearts ask; set it down here.",
      qa: [
        { q: "Why would Allah let an innocent boy be betrayed, sold, and imprisoned for years?",
          a: "The surah answers inside its own story: Allah is predominant over His affair, but most people do not know (12:21). Every hand that wronged Yusuf, brothers, traders, an accusing household, was unknowingly carrying him to the one place from which he could save nations and his own family. The injustice was never the plan failing; it was the road the plan used, and the wrongdoers still answer for their choices." },
        { q: "Yusuf did everything right and still lost years in prison. What is the lesson?",
          a: "That innocence does not exempt a soul from trial; it changes what the trial produces. He chose prison over sin and asked Allah for rescue from his own inclination (12:33), and the cell became a school of tawhid until vindication came publicly, before release. The Prophet ﷺ honoured that endurance, saying he himself would have answered the summoner sooner than Yusuf did (Bukhari 3372)." },
        { q: "How could Yusuf forgive brothers who threw him in a well?",
          a: "From the summit of total power he said: no blame upon you today; Allah will forgive you (12:92), asking his Lord to complete what he had begun. Forgiveness at full strength is the mark of one who sees Allah's hand in his story: under the decree, even envy had become the road to reunion. Centuries later the Prophet ﷺ reached for these very words over defeated Makkah." }
      ]
    },

    "n:19": {
      intro: "Pharaoh's last words trouble many readers; ask about them here.",
      qa: [
        { q: "Why was Pharaoh's belief refused when he finally said it?",
          a: "Because faith counts while it is still a choice about the unseen; when drowning overtook him, belief had become mere description of what his eyes could no longer deny (10:90-91). Years of signs had been answered with: I am your lord, most high, and the confession came only when choosing was over. The Prophet ﷺ set the same boundary for every soul: repentance is accepted until the soul reaches the throat (Tirmidhi 3537)." },
        { q: "Doesn't refusing a dying man's faith contradict Allah's mercy?",
          a: "The mercy was the years of signs, the gentle words Musa was commanded to speak, and every plague lifted after every broken promise. Even the refusal became mercy for others: today We preserve you in your body, that you may be a sign for those after you (10:92), a permanent warning to every throne that forgets it is mortal. And the door Pharaoh wasted stands open for every living person until the last breath." },
        { q: "Is Pharaoh's preserved body the mummy in a museum today?",
          a: "The Qur'an guarantees this much: his body was saved as a sign for those after him (10:92), and the people who had worshipped him needed to see the god drowned. Revelation does not name a particular mummy, so we hold the verse with certainty and leave modern identifications as possibility, not creed. Either way the lesson stands: the empire sank, and the body surfaced as a warning." }
      ]
    },

    "n:20": {
      intro: "Sinai's forty nights hold hard questions about seeing Allah and about the calf; ask them plainly.",
      qa: [
        { q: "Musa asked to see Allah. Was that wrong, and can Allah be seen?",
          a: "The desire was not scolded; the impossibility in this life was demonstrated: the mountain was levelled by the manifestation and Musa fell unconscious (7:143). No eye sees Him in this world, and the longing itself is worship, as this chapter says. What Sinai could not bear is promised to the people of Jannah: faces that Day radiant, looking at their Lord (75:22-23)." },
        { q: "How could a nation that walked through the split sea worship a calf forty nights later?",
          a: "Because miracles compel the eye, not the heart: when revelation fell silent in their hearing, they demanded something visible to serve. The Qur'an keeps the scene as a permanent mirror: the calf you can burn was never a god, and every generation melts its own jewelry into something it then obeys. Yet the same passage opens the answer wide: My mercy encompasses all things (7:156), and even the calf-worshippers were called back to repentance." },
        { q: "Musa threw down the Tablets and seized his brother. Doesn't such anger diminish a prophet?",
          a: "The Qur'an does not soften his fury, because fury at shirk is the right response of a heart that loves Allah; and then it teaches the discipline: when the anger subsided in Musa, he took up the Tablets. Anger comes, anger must pass, and revelation is picked up again. Harun was heard and cleared, and the nation was led back; that is the template for every leader after him." }
      ]
    },

    "n:23": {
      intro: "Isa's birth, his miracles, and verse 4:157 deserve unhurried answers; ask here.",
      qa: [
        { q: "What does the Qur'an mean when it says Isa was not crucified?",
          a: "Surah an-Nisa states it directly: they did not kill him, nor did they crucify him, but it was made to resemble so to them; rather, Allah raised him to Himself (4:157-158). The mainstream reading is that Allah cast the resemblance elsewhere and took His prophet up alive; classical commentators differ over the details of the resemblance, never over the outcome. His story is not finished: this Codex closes it at the white minaret." },
        { q: "If Isa had no father and raised the dead, why don't Muslims call him divine?",
          a: "The Qur'an answers with a parallel: the likeness of Isa with Allah is the likeness of Adam, created from dust, then told: Be, and he was (3:59). A miracle of origin proves the Creator's power, not the creature's divinity, and every one of his miracles came, in the Qur'an's constant refrain, by Allah's permission. His own first recorded words settle it from the cradle: I am the servant of Allah (19:30)." },
        { q: "Do Muslims love Isa and Maryam?",
          a: "Belief in Isa is part of iman itself, and the Prophet ﷺ said: I am the nearest of people to Isa ibn Maryam; the prophets are brothers (Bukhari 3443). Maryam is named in the Qur'an as chosen and purified above the women of the worlds (3:42), and mother and son were uniquely shielded from Shaytan's touch at birth (Bukhari 3431). Love for them is not a courtesy; it is creed." }
      ]
    },

    "n:26": {
      intro: "Why heaven defended a House surrounded by idols is a fair question; ask it.",
      qa: [
        { q: "Why would Allah defend the Kaaba when Makkah was full of idols?",
          a: "He was defending His own House and His own plan, not the religion of its neighbours: as Abdul-Muttalib said, the House has a Lord who will defend it. Ibrahim had raised it for tawhid, and in the very year of its defence its final purifier was born. The idols were trespassers soon to be removed; the House was never theirs." },
        { q: "Did birds really destroy an army?",
          a: "The Qur'an states it as an address to the eyewitnesses' own children: have you not seen how your Lord dealt with the companions of the elephant? (105:1). The Arabs dated their calendar by that year, which is not how legends behave. The army no tribe could face was ended by birds in flocks and stones of baked clay, so the lesson would be unmistakably heaven's." },
        { q: "What does an event before Islam have to do with Islam?",
          a: "It cleared and dated the stage: the Prophet ﷺ was born that same year by the dominant reckoning, and decades later he cited the Elephant in law on the day Makkah opened: Allah held back the elephant from Makkah, and its sanctity today is as its sanctity yesterday (Bukhari 112). The Elephant is the hinge between Jahiliyya and the Seerah: proof that the Lord of the House needed no believers to defend it while He prepared a believer the House would defend." }
      ]
    },

    "n:30": {
      intro: "The cave, the embrace, and the fear: ask what puzzles you about the first revelation.",
      qa: [
        { q: "What exactly is wahy, and how did revelation come to him?",
          a: "Wahy is Allah's own speech sent down, not a feeling arising within the Prophet's ﷺ mind; he described its two modes himself: sometimes like the ringing of a bell, which was hardest, and sometimes the angel speaking as a man (Bukhari 2). It arrived with physical weight, sweat on a bitterly cold day, as Aisha witnessed, for the Qur'an calls itself a heavy word (73:5). What he retained, he retained exactly, and it never became light across twenty-three years." },
        { q: "Why was the Prophet ﷺ terrified if the visitor was an angel?",
          a: "Because nothing had prepared him: the seizure in the cave was overwhelming, unannounced, and outside all human experience, and fear was the honest response of a truthful man. Khadijah read the fear rightly and answered it with evidence: Allah would never disgrace a man who keeps kinship, carries the weak, and helps against the calamities of truth (Bukhari 3). His terror is itself a proof that this was not a message he expected, sought, or invented." },
        { q: "Why did revelation stop for a while after the first verses?",
          a: "The pause, the fatrah, turned terror into longing: the heart that had fled the cave began to ache for what had frightened it. When revelation returned, comfort was overturned into commission: O you who wraps himself, arise and warn (74:1-2). The silence taught him, and every believer after him, that Allah's timing is part of Allah's message." }
      ]
    },

    "n:31": {
      intro: "Ta'if is the Seerah's most wounded page; ask what you need to about it.",
      qa: [
        { q: "Why did the Prophet ﷺ refuse to let the mountains crush Ta'if?",
          a: "Bleeding, he answered the Angel of the Mountains: rather, I hope that Allah will bring forth from their loins those who will worship Allah alone (Bukhari 3231). He measured a people by their unborn children, not by their worst afternoon. Within a generation Ta'if was Muslim, and its grandchildren filled the mosques he had hoped for." },
        { q: "Was his prayer in the orchard despair?",
          a: "It was complaint to Allah, never complaint about Him: O Allah, to You I complain of my weakness. Its one anxiety was neither revenge nor failure but whether the day signified his Lord's displeasure: if You are not angry with me, I do not care. The tradition keeps it as the du'a of the crushed: bring your brokenness to the One who does not tire of you." },
        { q: "What good came out of a journey that failed?",
          a: "The day's harvest was hidden: a Christian slave named Addas believed over a bunch of grapes, and on the road home a company of the jinn heard the Qur'an at Nakhlah and returned to their people as warners (72:1). When one city refuses, heaven has other audiences already seated. And on the heels of the hardest year came the highest journey, the Isra." }
      ]
    },

    "n:32": {
      intro: "A journey through the heavens invites honest questions; ask them here.",
      qa: [
        { q: "Was the Isra and Mi'raj in body, or only in spirit?",
          a: "The early majority held, and mainstream teaching still holds, that it was in body and soul, while awake; the Qur'an says He took His servant by night (17:1), and a servant is soul and body together. Some later voices read it as a vision of the soul, a minority position the scholars record without alarm. The practical fruit is untouched by the debate: the five prayers came down from that night." },
        { q: "How is such a journey physically possible?",
          a: "The verse begins with the answer: glory be to Him who took His servant by night (17:1); the subject of the sentence is Allah, not the speed of the traveler. The One who created distance is not bound by it. That is why the report sorted Makkah overnight: those seeking a reason to leave found one, and Abu Bakr earned the name as-Siddiq by believing before hearing the details." },
        { q: "Why were fifty prayers reduced to five? Did Allah change His mind?",
          a: "Allah knew from eternity that five would be the final number; the returning and asking, at Musa's counsel, taught the ummah mercy, intercession, and the worth of what it received: five in practice, fifty in reward. The exchange also fixed prayer's origin above the Lote Tree, prescribed in the highest nearness rather than devised on earth. Every adhan since is an echo of that night." }
      ]
    },

    "n:39": {
      intro: "A defeat with the Prophet ﷺ present raises real questions; bring them here.",
      qa: [
        { q: "If Allah helped at Badr, why did the Muslims lose at Uhud?",
          a: "The Qur'an answers without softening: Allah fulfilled His promise while you were cutting them down by His permission, until you weakened, disputed the command, and disobeyed (3:152). Help is tied to obedience, not to identity, and the archers' abandoned hill remains the standing lesson. Such days are rotated among people (3:140), so that faith learns what it is made of." },
        { q: "Does the defeat mean Allah was displeased with the believers?",
          a: "The same verse that names the failure announces the pardon: and He certainly pardoned you (3:152). The day purified the ranks, exposed the hypocrites' hesitation, and took martyrs whom the Prophet ﷺ honoured; Anas ibn an-Nadr smelled Jannah's fragrance from beyond Uhud (Bukhari 2805). Loss under Allah's eye is a lesson, not an abandonment: the wounded community marched out again the very next morning." },
        { q: "Why was the Prophet ﷺ himself wounded if Allah protects His messengers?",
          a: "Allah's protection guaranteed the message's completion, not exemption from pain: his tooth was broken, blood ran on his face, and the mission continued from that same field. The chapter puts it plainly: prophethood does not mean exemption from wound, only that the wound is not the end of the mission. His scars are part of what he carried for the people he was sent to." }
      ]
    },

    "n:40": {
      intro: "The trench, the fear, and what followed it are worth asking about.",
      qa: [
        { q: "How was a siege of ten thousand broken without a battle?",
          a: "By a ditch, by discord, and by wind: Salman's counsel shaped the ground, Nu'aym ibn Mas'ud quietly unstitched the alliance, and then Allah sent against them a wind and armies you did not see (33:9). The believers' part was hunger, digging, and holding the line; the ending was heaven's. When the confederates scattered, the Prophet ﷺ announced the turning of the tide: now we raid them, and they will not raid us (Bukhari 4110)." },
        { q: "What happened with Banu Qurayzah, and why so severely?",
          a: "During the siege they broke their pact with Madinah and turned hostile on the city's exposed flank at its most dangerous hour: an act of war in a war of survival. Afterward they accepted judgment by Sa'd ibn Mu'adh, the chief of their own former allies, and his verdict followed the law their own tradition applied to wartime treachery (Muslim 1768). The sirah treats it as a distinct chapter, and so does this Codex: a breach judged in its time, not a verdict on any people for all time." },
        { q: "Does digging a trench show weak trust in Allah?",
          a: "The opposite: taking sound means is part of tawakkul, and accepting a companion's good idea is part of prophetic leadership, as this chapter says of Salman's counsel. The Prophet ﷺ dug with his own hands, a stone bound against his stomach from hunger. Means were taken to their limit, and then the wind came; both halves belong to trust." }
      ]
    },

    "n:46": {
      intro: "The hardest morning of the ummah still teaches; ask what you carry.",
      qa: [
        { q: "How did the community survive the Prophet's ﷺ death?",
          a: "On two sentences and a verse: whoever worshipped Muhammad, Muhammad has died; and whoever worships Allah, Allah is Living and does not die, followed by: Muhammad is only a messenger; messengers have passed away before him (3:144), the verse Abu Bakr recited to the stunned mosque (Bukhari 4452). The religion had been built to outlive its Messenger, with the Book, the Sunnah, and a community trained to stand at Fajr even while weeping. Grief was permitted at its fullest, and the line did not break." },
        { q: "Why did Allah let His beloved Prophet ﷺ die like other men?",
          a: "Because he was a servant, and servanthood was his honour: indeed, you will die, and indeed, they will die (39:30) was revealed years before. Offered the choice at the end, he chose the Highest Companion, ar-Rafiq al-A'la (Bukhari 4463). A prophet who never died might have been worshipped; his death guards the tawhid he taught." },
        { q: "Did the Prophet ﷺ name a successor?",
          a: "He left no written appointment, but in his final illness he insisted three times, over objections: order Abu Bakr to lead the people in prayer (Bukhari 664), and the community read the imamate of prayer as the quiet designation, as this chapter recounts. The Companions then pledged to Abu Bakr openly, and Sunni teaching holds his caliphate legitimate by that consensus. The Prophet's ﷺ own gesture and the ummah's agreement answered the question together." }
      ]
    },

    "n:50": {
      intro: "The wars between Companions grieve every believing heart; ask how to hold them.",
      qa: [
        { q: "How should Sunnis view battles like the Camel and Siffin?",
          a: "Through the verses revealed ready for them: if two parties of the believers fight, make peace between them, and: the believers are but brothers (49:9-10); both camps remained believers, and neither left Islam. The adab the imams fixed is love for all the Companions, prayers of mercy for all who fought, and a held tongue where the swords once were: that is a nation that has passed; theirs is what they earned (2:134). We were not commanded to reopen their wounds, only to learn their lessons." },
        { q: "Was Ali in the right, and were those who fought him disbelievers?",
          a: "Ahl as-Sunnah hold that Ali was the rightful caliph and the closer of the two sides to the truth; the Prophet ﷺ had said of Ammar, who fell beside him, that the transgressing party would kill him (Muslim 2915). Yet those who opposed him were believers who erred in judgment, not hypocrites or apostates, and the Camel ended with Ali escorting the Mother of the Believers home with full honour, her regret and his reverence both on the record. Right and wrong existed, and brotherhood survived both." },
        { q: "Who were the Khawarij, and why do they still matter?",
          a: "They were zealots who made takfir a weapon, declaring sinners disbelievers, and the Prophet ﷺ foretold them precisely: they pass through the religion as the arrow passes through the prey (Bukhari 6930). Ali taught the ummah how to classify such people forever: not disbelievers, for from disbelief they fled, but brothers who transgressed against us. Every age that watches zeal outrun knowledge is looking at their shadow." }
      ]
    },

    "n:51": {
      intro: "How the Book and the Sunnah reached you intact is worth asking about.",
      qa: [
        { q: "How do we know the Qur'an today is exactly what was revealed?",
          a: "By double custody: a written text gathered under Abu Bakr and unified under Uthman, and an unbroken relay of memorizers from the Companions' circles to the millions of huffaz alive now, each side checking the other every Ramadan. Transmission this massive and continuous is called mutawatir: fabrication would have required every land and generation to conspire in the same error at once. Behind the human diligence stands the promise: indeed, We sent down the Reminder, and indeed We are its Guardian (15:9)." },
        { q: "How can hadith collected generations later be trusted?",
          a: "They were not gathered from rumour but through the isnad: named, dated, biographied transmitters, with a critical science grading every carrier's memory and honesty. Ibn al-Mubarak said: the isnad is part of the religion; were it not for the isnad, whoever wished would have said whatever he wished (Muqaddimah of Sahih Muslim). Al-Bukhari sifted six hundred thousand reports across sixteen years to accept about seven thousand, and the grades sahih, hasan, and da'if exist precisely because the scholars refused to hide a report's weakness." },
        { q: "What keeps the religion from drifting over the centuries?",
          a: "The guard-sciences built around the two revelations: Arabic grammar codified so the Book's tongue could not drift, usul al-fiqh so law is derived rather than improvised, and the four madhhabs as disciplined roads to one qiblah, each imam saying in his own words: if the hadith is authentic, that is my madhhab. And the Prophet ﷺ promised living renewal: Allah will raise for this ummah, at the head of every century, one who renews for it its religion (Abu Dawud 4291)." }
      ]
    },

    "n:53": {
      intro: "Signs of the Hour can frighten more than they teach; ask for their right measure.",
      qa: [
        { q: "So many signs seem fulfilled. Should I be afraid?",
          a: "The chapter's own grammar is the answer: signs are mercy before they are threat, the Hour announcing itself in installments so that no soul can say it was ambushed. Their purpose is readiness, not dread: prayer kept, wrongs repented, provision earned honestly. Every generation sees enough signs to wake and few do; be of the few, and fear finds little room in a heart busy obeying." },
        { q: "Can anyone work out when the Hour will come?",
          a: "No. Its knowledge is with my Lord; none reveals its time but He (7:187), and when Jibril himself asked, the Prophet ﷺ answered that the one asked knows no more than the asker (Muslim 8). Date-setting has embarrassed every generation that tried it; the signs were given for watchfulness, not arithmetic. What is certain is the suddenness: for its signs have already come (47:18)." },
        { q: "Which signs have already happened?",
          a: "The first was the sending of the Prophet ﷺ himself: I and the Hour have been sent like these two (Bukhari 6504); then his death, the opening of Jerusalem, plagues, abundance of wealth, and a line of false prophets, thirty dajjals foretold (Bukhari 3609). Others unfold before every generation's eyes, like the barefoot shepherds competing in tall buildings (Muslim 8). Some still wait, like the Euphrates uncovering a mountain of gold (Bukhari 7119) and the land of the Arabs returning to meadows and rivers (Muslim 157)." }
      ]
    },

    "n:54": {
      intro: "Claims about the Mahdi confuse many; ask for what is sound.",
      qa: [
        { q: "Is the Mahdi authentic Islamic teaching or later legend?",
          a: "Belief in the Mahdi is the settled position of Ahl as-Sunnah, resting on narrations in the sunan graded sound by the hadith masters: a man of the Prophet's ﷺ house who fills the earth with justice as it was filled with wrong (Abu Dawud 4285). The Sahihayn do not use the title, but they carry his description: your imam from among you when the son of Maryam descends (Bukhari 3449), and the army swallowed by the earth at al-Bayda (Muslim 2882). The tradition states its own evidence honestly, and that honesty is part of why it can be trusted." },
        { q: "With so many claimants in history, how would the true Mahdi be known?",
          a: "By what he is, not by what he claims: of Fatimah's line, bearing the Prophet's ﷺ name, set right by Allah in a single night, and known by justice and humility rather than by campaigns (Abu Dawud 4284). This chapter's measure is the morning he steps back from his own mihrab to offer the prayer to Isa; every claimant who reached for a throne has disproved himself by the reaching. Even the counterfeits were foretold, thirty dajjals claiming what is not theirs (Bukhari 3609), so their existence confirms the teaching rather than shaking it." },
        { q: "What will the Mahdi actually do?",
          a: "Unite the believers in the generation of the Dajjal, rule seven years in which the earth fills with fairness, and give wealth without counting it (Abu Dawud 4285). He works no required miracle and does not descend from the sky; he is a man whom Allah rectifies for a task. In his era Isa descends and prays behind this ummah's imam (Bukhari 3449), and the last just rule of history ends its first morning with a king trying to give away his place." }
      ]
    },

    "n:55": {
      intro: "The Dajjal is the trial most warned of; ask how to stand before you ever see it.",
      qa: [
        { q: "Why would Allah allow a deceiver with such powers?",
          a: "There is no trial greater between the creation of Adam and the Hour (Muslim 2946), and it comes as the final examination of what every earlier trial rehearsed: whether the heart judges by the eye or by revelation. His props invert appearances, for his fire is a cool garden and his garden is a fire (Muslim 2934), so only those anchored in creed pass: your Lord is not one-eyed (Bukhari 7131). And the trial is strictly bounded: forty days, two cities barred to him, and an ending already written at the gate of Ludd." },
        { q: "How do I protect myself and my family from him?",
          a: "The protection was prescribed fourteen centuries early: memorize the opening verses of Surat al-Kahf (Muslim 809), seek refuge in the last part of every prayer from the trial of the Masih ad-Dajjal (Muslim 588), and if he emerges in your lifetime, keep far from him (Abu Dawud 4319). Above all, hold the creed that unmasks him: no one sees his Lord until he dies (Muslim 169). A believer drilled in these is armed before the enemy ever arrives." },
        { q: "Is the Dajjal alive somewhere right now?",
          a: "The hadith of al-Jassasah, which the Prophet ﷺ retold from the minbar, describes him shackled on an island, asking sailors about the signs of his release (Muslim 2942). Where and when are Allah's knowledge, and searching maps for him is not the preparation the Prophet ﷺ prescribed. Our assignment is readiness of creed and prayer, not geography." }
      ]
    },

    "n:56": {
      intro: "How Isa returns while prophethood is sealed is a fair question; ask it here.",
      qa: [
        { q: "If Muhammad ﷺ is the seal of the prophets, how can Isa come back?",
          a: "The seal (33:40) means no new prophet is raised after him, and Isa's return breaks nothing of that: he was made a prophet centuries before, and he descends with no new revelation and no new law. He returns as a just ruler of this ummah, judging by this Qur'an, and prays behind its imam: your imam is from among you (Bukhari 3449). His descent honours the finality rather than contradicting it: even a prophet returns as a follower of Muhammad's ﷺ way." },
        { q: "What will Isa do when he descends?",
          a: "He descends at the white minaret east of Damascus at Fajr, ends the Dajjal at the gate of Ludd (Muslim 2937), and then dismantles the symbols of his own deification: he will break the cross, kill the swine, and abolish the jizyah (Bukhari 2222). His era is the earth's sabbath: security, overflowing wealth, and rancor lifted. He remains forty years, dies as every son of Adam dies, and the Muslims pray the janazah over him (Abu Dawud 4324)." },
        { q: "Why Isa, of all prophets, at the end?",
          a: "Because history's greatest dispute is about him, and Allah lets him settle it in person: the one they claimed died on a cross returns to end the one claiming to be god, and there is none of the People of the Book but will believe in him before his death (4:159). His janazah is the gentlest refutation ever staged: the worshipped one is prayed over and buried in the earth like his father Adam. The end of time closes the argument the middle of time could not." }
      ]
    },

    "n:57": {
      intro: "Yajuj and Majuj sit strangely on the modern map; ask what the sources actually say.",
      qa: [
        { q: "Where are Yajuj and Majuj now, and why can't satellites find the barrier?",
          a: "Revelation affirms the event, not the address: Dhul-Qarnayn sealed them behind iron and molten copper (18:96), and their release comes near the true promise (21:96), but no verse gives coordinates, and the unseen is under no obligation to appear on our maps. The Prophet ﷺ was shown the first crack in his own lifetime (Bukhari 3346), a sign of nearness, not a location. The believer's task is to trust the promise; the surveying was never assigned to us." },
        { q: "Are Yajuj and Majuj human beings?",
          a: "Yes: the tradition counts them among the children of Adam, and on the Day they supply the great mass of the delegation of the Fire, nine hundred ninety-nine of every thousand (Bukhari 3348). They are not monsters of myth but humanity at its most numberless and most heedless. Their arrows shot at the sky are, as this chapter puts it, the creature's arithmetic at its most complete and most wrong." },
        { q: "How can an unstoppable horde be defeated?",
          a: "No army engages them: Allah tells Isa, I have brought out servants of Mine against whom no one has power to fight, so gather My servants to at-Tur (Muslim 2937). The believers turn to du'a, and Allah ends the horde in one night with worms in their necks, then sends birds and a washing rain until the earth is left like a mirror. Scale has never once been the operative variable; that is the whole lesson." }
      ]
    },

    "n:58": {
      intro: "A sunrise that closes repentance deserves a careful question; ask it now.",
      qa: [
        { q: "Why does repentance stop being accepted after the western sunrise?",
          a: "Because tawbah is a turning toward the unseen, and that morning the unseen is over: when the people see it, all of them together will believe, and that is when belief will not benefit a soul that did not believe before (6:158). Faith declared under total proof is no longer a choice; it is surrender to the visible, like Pharaoh's word under the wave. The closure is not mercy tiring; it is the test ending on schedule, announced fourteen centuries in advance (Bukhari 4635)." },
        { q: "If I repented before that day, does my faith still count?",
          a: "Yes, fully: what stops benefitting is new belief and new earning of good, not the faith and deeds already in place. The Prophet ﷺ said: whoever repents before the sun rises from its west, Allah accepts his repentance (Muslim 2703), and until then His hand is extended by night for the day's sinner and by day for the night's (Muslim 2759). The verse is a deadline for the delayer, not a threat to the repentant." },
        { q: "Is the sun rising from the west literal?",
          a: "Mainstream teaching takes it as a real event, the first of the heavenly signs: the sun that nightly prostrates beneath the Throne and asks leave will one morning be told, return whence you came (Bukhari 3199). The earlier great signs ran through creatures and could be argued with; this one is staged in the sky, where no deception reaches. Its practical meaning is one word, as this chapter says: now." }
      ]
    },

    "n:59": {
      intro: "A creature that speaks is a strange promise; ask how to hold it.",
      qa: [
        { q: "Am I really required to believe in a talking beast?",
          a: "The verse is explicit: We will bring forth for them a creature from the earth, speaking to them (27:82), so its reality is Qur'anic text, not folklore. What is required is certainty about the event with restraint about the anatomy: Ibn Kathir's honest summary, which this chapter follows, is that the Qur'an withheld the details on purpose. A believer affirms what was said and does not invent what was not." },
        { q: "What will the Dabba actually do?",
          a: "It announces the verdict of 27:82: mankind's uncertainty about the signs is over, because a sign is now speaking with them. Reports in the sunan add that it marks people, the believer's face brightened and the denier stamped; those narrations sit below the Sahihayn in grade, as this chapter notes, but their meaning matches the verse's function. Its timing is welded to the western sunrise: whichever of the two comes first, the other follows close behind (Muslim 2941)." },
        { q: "Why would Allah use a creature as a sign?",
          a: "Each age is answered in its own mode of denial, and an age that trusts only what it can examine is given a specimen it cannot classify, speaking a language it cannot dismiss. By then the era of persuasion has closed: the Dabba does not argue, it announces. The living mercy is that the announcement's content is being written now, choice by choice, on every heart." }
      ]
    },

    "n:64": {
      intro: "The Trumpet ends and begins everything; ask what happens to us in between.",
      qa: [
        { q: "What happens at the first blast, and who is excepted?",
          a: "At the first blast all in the heavens and the earth fall dead, except whom Allah wills (39:68); revelation leaves the excepted unnamed, and the scholars offered suggestions while withholding certainty where the text withholds it. Before that day a gentle wind will have gathered the soul of every believer (Muslim 2937), so the terror falls on a world the faithful have already left. The bearer of the Horn has been leaning toward the command since creation (Tirmidhi 2431); every plan we make is drafted inside that held breath." },
        { q: "What happens to souls between the two blasts?",
          a: "The dead remain in the barzakh, the interval where every soul already waits between death and resurrection, and the gap itself is measured in a unit deliberately withheld: forty, said Abu Hurayrah, declining to say days, months, or years (Bukhari 4935). In that interval a rain descends and the bodies sprout as herbs sprout, rebuilt from the one bone that does not decay. Then the second blast, and at once they are standing, looking on (39:68)." },
        { q: "Will the world end gradually or all at once?",
          a: "All at once, mid-gesture: the Hour rises while two men spread a garment and cannot finish the sale, while a man lifts the morsel to his mouth and cannot eat it (Bukhari 6506). Nothing in that suddenness is aimed at the believer, whose soul is taken earlier in gentleness by the wind. The lesson this chapter draws is practical: finish the deed that should not be interrupted." }
      ]
    },

    "n:65": {
      intro: "Bodies returning from dust invite honest wonder; ask how it can be.",
      qa: [
        { q: "How can bodies scattered for centuries be brought back?",
          a: "The Qur'an argues from the first assembly: does man think We will not gather his bones? Rather, We are able to reshape his fingertips (75:3-4), naming the one feature later ages would learn is never repeated. He who built you from a drop once has kept the pattern, and He rehearses in public every spring when the dead land revives. As We began the first creation, We repeat it: a promise, and a proof already performed." },
        { q: "What is the coccyx hadith about?",
          a: "The Prophet ﷺ taught that everything of the human body decays except one bone, ajb adh-dhanab at the base of the spine, and from it the creation is assembled on the Day (Bukhari 4935). The species first built from clay keeps a seed for the rebuild, watered by a rain sent between the blasts. We affirm it as taught and leave its biology to the One who wrote it." },
        { q: "In what state will we be raised?",
          a: "As we first arrived: barefoot, naked, and uncircumcised, creation restored to its beginning (Bukhari 6527). Aisha asked whether men and women would look at one another, and the answer measures the Day: the matter is more severe than that. Honour returns in order, and mercifully: Ibrahim, once stripped for the fire, is the first to be clothed (Bukhari 3349)." }
      ]
    },

    "n:66": {
      intro: "Fifty thousand years of standing is a heavy thought; ask how the believer stands it.",
      qa: [
        { q: "Is the 50,000-year day the same length for everyone?",
          a: "The measure is real: a Day whose measure is fifty thousand years (70:4), but its weight bends with the state of the heart; the hadith this chapter quotes promises that it is lightened for the believer until it is easier than one obligatory prayer he prayed in the world. The sweat rises by deeds, to the ankles, the knees, the waist, or the bridle (Muslim 2864). The Standing is long for the one whose life made it long." },
        { q: "How does anyone survive the sun brought so near?",
          a: "By shade, and the shade's conditions were published centuries early: seven will Allah shade on the Day there is no shade but His, a just ruler, a youth raised in worship, a heart attached to the mosques, two who loved for Allah's sake, chastity under invitation, hidden charity, and solitary tears of remembrance (Bukhari 660). An added clause covers the one who eases a hard-pressed debtor (Muslim 3006). The Day's shelter is moral, and its gate list is already public; join a category tonight." },
        { q: "Is there any help or intercession on that day?",
          a: "Yes: when the Standing becomes unbearable, mankind goes from prophet to prophet, Adam to Nuh to Ibrahim to Musa to Isa, each saying: myself, myself, until the plea reaches Muhammad ﷺ, who says: I am for it (Bukhari 4712), and prostrates until judgment is opened. Intercession is real, by Allah's permission, and its greatest holder is the one sent as mercy. The believer has also rehearsed this plain before: every Arafat was practice for the Mawqif." }
      ]
    },

    "n:69": {
      intro: "The reckoning raises the deepest questions of fairness; ask them without fear.",
      qa: [
        { q: "Is the reckoning fair to people who never heard the message?",
          a: "The Qur'an lays the foundation: We never punish until We have sent a messenger (17:15), and no soul is wronged the weight of a mustard seed (21:47). For those the call never truly reached, many scholars hold, on the strength of reports in Musnad Ahmad, that they will be tested on the Day itself, so that no one is condemned without the argument standing against him. And the Judge is more merciful to His servants than a mother to her child (Bukhari 5999); fairness is not merely kept that Day, it is perfected." },
        { q: "Will my sins be exposed in front of everyone?",
          a: "For the believer, the hope is the private audience: Allah brings him near, covers him with His screen, has him acknowledge sin after sin, then says: I concealed it for you in the world, and I forgive it for you today (Bukhari 2441). What ruins is strict examination: whoever's account is examined is destroyed, and the easy account is the presentation, not the interrogation (Bukhari 6536). So the believer's plea is not a spotless past but a covering mercy." },
        { q: "What will matter most on the Scale?",
          a: "The prayer is the first deed of the servant to be weighed, and blood the first case judged between people; good character is the heaviest single deposit, and two words are light on the tongue, heavy on the Scale: Subhan Allah wa bi-hamdih, Subhan Allah al-Azim (Bukhari 7563). A single card bearing the shahada outweighs ninety-nine scrolls of sin (Tirmidhi 2639), because nothing outweighs the name of Allah. Weigh your days by what the Scale will weigh." }
      ]
    },

    "n:70": {
      intro: "The bridge over the Fire concentrates every hope and fear; ask about the crossing.",
      qa: [
        { q: "What determines how fast a person crosses the Sirat?",
          a: "Deeds set the speed: the believer crosses like the blink of an eye, like lightning, like wind, like birds, like the finest riders, while hooks like the thorns of Sa'dan snatch people by their deeds (Muslim 183). Light is issued for the crossing, and it is earned now: their light running before them (57:12), fed by wudu, prayer, and the Qur'an. Your crossing speed is being set today, in ordinary daily constancy." },
        { q: "Does the verse saying none of you but will arrive at it mean everyone touches the Fire?",
          a: "The arrival is universal: a matter decreed, then We save those who feared Him (19:71-72); the crossing is not. The mainstream reading is that the arrival is the crossing itself, over the bridge laid across the Fire's back, though some early scholars read the arrival more broadly; salvation, either way, is the promised second clause. For the God-fearing the Fire is passed over, not entered." },
        { q: "What is the Qantara after the bridge?",
          a: "An arch between the bridge and the Garden where the saved are halted and the wrongs still standing between believers are settled, until, refined and purified, they are given leave to enter (Bukhari 6535). No unresolved grievance walks through Jannah's gate. The chapter's counsel is tender and urgent: forgive your brother tonight, or the arch will settle it later." }
      ]
    },

    "n:71": {
      intro: "Even the Garden raises questions; ask them with a glad heart.",
      qa: [
        { q: "Won't eternity in Jannah eventually become boring?",
          a: "Boredom is a wound of this world, where joys repeat, fade, and fall short of what the heart was made for; Jannah is defined as what no eye has seen, no ear has heard, and no human heart has conceived (Bukhari 3244). Its delight increases rather than dulls: a market every Jumu'ah where beauty itself is renewed, and above everything the vision of the Lord, a joy that cannot age (75:22-23). The heart was built for Him, and there it is finally home." },
        { q: "Will I be with my family and the people I love?",
          a: "The Qur'an promises reunion across the ranks: We join their descendants to them, and We deprive them of nothing of their deeds (52:21), and the Prophet ﷺ said: a person is with those he loves (Bukhari 6168). Rancor is drawn out at the door (15:47), so what remains between loved ones is only the love. Ranks differ, and mercy joins what rank would separate." },
        { q: "What is the greatest reward in Jannah?",
          a: "Two gifts above the gardens themselves: the announcement, I bestow upon you My pleasure, and I will never be angry with you after it, forever (Bukhari 6549), and the vision: faces that Day radiant, looking at their Lord (75:22-23), as you see the full moon, without crowding (Bukhari 554). What Musa was denied at the mountain is granted to the least believer at home. And when you ask, ask for Firdaws; he ﷺ gave us the address (Bukhari 2790)." }
      ]
    },

    "c:j-iblis": {
      intro: "The enemy is named so he can be understood; ask about him plainly.",
      qa: [
        { q: "Was Iblis an angel?",
          a: "No: the Qur'an states he was of the jinn (18:50), a being of smokeless fire who worshipped among the angels, which is why the command to prostrate reached him. Angels do not disobey what Allah commands them (66:6), and his refusal is itself the proof of his kind; a few early commentators counted him among the angels, but the verse settles the mainstream position. His sin was pride: I am better than him (7:12), the first argument from origin instead of obedience." },
        { q: "Can Shaytan force me to sin?",
          a: "He cannot: his tools are whisper and false promise, for he promises them nothing but delusion (17:64), and his own speech on the Day disowns every excuse: I had no authority over you except that I called you and you responded, so do not blame me; blame yourselves (14:22). Every victory of his requires the victim's signature. The Qur'an's verdict is contempt, not terror: the plot of Shaytan is ever weak (4:76)." },
        { q: "Why did Allah grant Iblis respite instead of destroying him?",
          a: "The respite he asked for was folded into the world's purpose: this life is a test, and a tester who cannot compel completes the test without breaking its fairness. The scholars noticed the geometry of his threat, from before, behind, right, and left (7:16-17): no approach from above, where du'a rises, nor from below, where the forehead lands in sujud. The two postures he cannot reach are the believer's open doors." }
      ]
    },

    "c:j-qarin": {
      intro: "The companion assigned to every soul raises uneasy questions; ask them here.",
      qa: [
        { q: "Does everyone really have a personal shaytan?",
          a: "Yes: there is no one among you but a companion from the jinn has been assigned to him, the Prophet ﷺ said, and when they asked, even you?, he answered: even me, but Allah helped me against him and he submitted, so he commands me with nothing but good (Muslim 2814). The assignment is universal and no cause for panic; it simply names where the whisper comes from, so the heart knows its ground." },
        { q: "Is the qarin a kind of possession?",
          a: "No: he is a whisperer stationed near, with no hand on your will, and on the Day he disowns his man: our Lord, I did not make him transgress; he was himself in far error (50:27). The prescribed defense is remembrance, not exorcism: the whisperer shrinks when Allah is remembered, as this entry says. If fear of the unseen begins to weigh on your daily life, keep dhikr close and speak with a trusted scholar or your local imam." },
        { q: "Do the dark whispers in my head count as sins?",
          a: "No: Allah has overlooked for this ummah what their souls whisper, so long as it is not acted upon or spoken (Muslim 127). When Companions were distressed by thoughts too terrible to utter, the Prophet ﷺ called that very distress clear faith (Muslim 132): only a believing heart is troubled by the whisper. The whisper is his; the resistance is yours, and the resistance is worship." }
      ]
    },

    "c:a-harut-marut": {
      intro: "Two angels and sorcery make a puzzling verse; ask what it means.",
      qa: [
        { q: "Why would angels teach magic at all?",
          a: "They were sent as a test, and they taught no one until they had warned: we are only a fitnah, so do not disbelieve (2:102). The teaching came fenced with its own warning, so that what people did next revealed their hearts; a warning delivered does not excuse the one who ignores it, as this entry says. Knowledge itself can be a trial, and Babylon's lesson is that a technique can cost a soul its share of the Hereafter." },
        { q: "Did Harut and Marut sin with a woman, as the famous stories say?",
          a: "Those tales are storytellers' additions with no sound chain, and the Qur'an contains none of it; the muhaddithun set them aside, and this Codex leaves them where the muhaddithun left them. What revelation actually reports is restrained: two angels, a test, a warning, and people who learned anyway. Angels do not disobey what Allah commands them (66:6), and the verse ascribes no sin to these two." },
        { q: "Does the verse mean magic is real, and can I ever go to someone who practices it?",
          a: "Real enough to harm, but only by Allah's permission (2:102), and the same verse prices it exactly: whoever purchased it has no share in the Hereafter. Sorcery is counted among the gravest sins, and seeking out its practitioners walks into the very trade the verse condemns. If you fear you or your family are touched by it, hold to the prescribed remembrances and refuge, and speak with a trusted scholar or your local imam." }
      ]
    }

  },

  glossary: [
    { term: "Isnad", aliases: ["chain", "chain of narration", "sanad"], def: "The chain of named transmitters through which a hadith reaches us; each carrier's honesty and memory is examined before the report is trusted." },
    { term: "Sahih", aliases: ["saheeh"], def: "Sound: the highest grade of hadith, earned by an unbroken chain of reliable transmitters and a text free of hidden defects." },
    { term: "Hasan", aliases: [], def: "Good: a hadith slightly below sahih, usually through a transmitter of lighter memory; still accepted as evidence." },
    { term: "Da'if", aliases: ["daif", "weak hadith"], def: "Weak: a hadith whose chain fails a condition; scholars flag such reports openly rather than pass them off as sound." },
    { term: "Mutawatir", aliases: [], def: "A report carried by so many independent chains in every generation that fabrication is impossible; the Qur'an is transmitted this way." },
    { term: "Sahihayn", aliases: ["the two Sahihs", "Bukhari and Muslim"], def: "The two most rigorously vetted hadith collections, Sahih al-Bukhari and Sahih Muslim." },
    { term: "Sunnah", aliases: ["sunna"], def: "The Prophet's ﷺ teaching, practice, and approvals; the lived explanation of the Qur'an." },
    { term: "Hadith", aliases: ["ahadith", "hadeeth"], def: "A transmitted report of what the Prophet ﷺ said, did, or approved, carried with its chain of narrators." },
    { term: "Seerah", aliases: ["sirah", "sira"], def: "The recorded biography of the Prophet ﷺ, from birth to passing." },
    { term: "Tafsir", aliases: ["tafseer"], def: "Scholarly explanation of the Qur'an's meanings, verse by verse." },
    { term: "Wahy", aliases: ["revelation"], def: "Revelation: Allah's communication to His prophets, brought to Muhammad ﷺ by the angel Jibril." },
    { term: "Tawhid", aliases: ["tawheed"], def: "The oneness of Allah: He alone is Lord, He alone is worshipped, and His names and attributes are His alone." },
    { term: "Shirk", aliases: [], def: "Associating anything with Allah in worship or in His unique rights; the gravest wrong in Islam." },
    { term: "Taqwa", aliases: ["God-consciousness"], def: "God-consciousness: living aware that Allah sees, guarding oneself from what displeases Him." },
    { term: "Iman", aliases: ["eman", "faith"], def: "Faith: belief in Allah, His angels, His books, His messengers, the Last Day, and the decree." },
    { term: "Ihsan", aliases: [], def: "Excellence in worship: to worship Allah as though you see Him, for though you do not see Him, He sees you." },
    { term: "Fitnah", aliases: ["fitna"], def: "Trial, temptation, or strife; anything that tests faith, from persecution to civil war to a whisper." },
    { term: "Ummah", aliases: ["umma"], def: "The worldwide community of Muslims, bound by faith rather than land or blood." },
    { term: "Qadar", aliases: ["qadr", "predestination", "decree"], def: "Allah's decree: His eternal knowledge and writing of all things before they occur." },
    { term: "Barzakh", aliases: [], def: "The interval between death and resurrection, where every soul waits until the Trumpet." },
    { term: "Akhirah", aliases: ["the Hereafter", "akhira"], def: "The everlasting life after this world: resurrection, judgment, and the final home." },
    { term: "Dunya", aliases: [], def: "This present world and its life, brief by design and weighed against the akhirah." },
    { term: "Jahiliyyah", aliases: ["jahiliyya", "Age of Ignorance"], def: "The age of idolatry and tribal darkness in Arabia before revelation." },
    { term: "Hanif", aliases: ["hunafa"], def: "One who turned from idols to the pure worship of the one God, as Ibrahim did." },
    { term: "Hijrah", aliases: ["hijra"], def: "The migration of the Prophet ﷺ and his followers from Makkah to Madinah; year one of the Islamic calendar." },
    { term: "Ansar", aliases: ["the Helpers"], def: "The Helpers: the Muslims of Madinah who sheltered and supported the emigrants from Makkah." },
    { term: "Muhajirun", aliases: ["muhajireen", "the Emigrants"], def: "The Emigrants: the Muslims who left Makkah and their possessions behind for Madinah." },
    { term: "Khalifah", aliases: ["caliph", "khalifa", "khilafah"], def: "Successor: the leader entrusted with the community after the Prophet ﷺ; also mankind's role as steward on earth." },
    { term: "Riddah", aliases: ["ridda"], def: "The wave of tribal apostasy across Arabia after the Prophet's ﷺ passing, met and ended under Abu Bakr." },
    { term: "Mushaf", aliases: ["mus'haf"], def: "A written copy of the Qur'an between two covers." },
    { term: "Qiblah", aliases: ["qibla"], def: "The direction of prayer, toward the Kaaba in Makkah." },
    { term: "Rak'ah", aliases: ["rakah", "rakat"], def: "One unit of the prayer: standing, bowing, and two prostrations." },
    { term: "Du'a", aliases: ["dua", "supplication"], def: "Personal supplication: speaking to Allah directly, in any language, at any time." },
    { term: "Dhikr", aliases: ["zikr", "remembrance"], def: "Remembrance of Allah with tongue and heart, in phrases the Prophet ﷺ taught." },
    { term: "Takbir", aliases: ["takbeer"], def: "Saying Allahu akbar, Allah is greatest; the phrase that opens the prayer." },
    { term: "Adhan", aliases: ["azan", "call to prayer"], def: "The public call to prayer, given five times daily." },
    { term: "Iqamah", aliases: ["iqama"], def: "The short second call announcing that the prayer is about to begin." },
    { term: "Mizan", aliases: ["the Scale"], def: "The Scale on which deeds are weighed on the Day of Resurrection, precise to a mustard seed." },
    { term: "Sirat", aliases: ["the Bridge", "as-Sirat"], def: "The bridge laid over Jahannam that every soul must cross; also the straight path of guidance." },
    { term: "Hawd", aliases: ["the Basin", "al-Kawthar"], def: "The Prophet's ﷺ Basin, fed by Kawthar, from which believers drink before Jannah and never thirst again." },
    { term: "Shafa'a", aliases: ["shafa'ah", "intercession"], def: "Intercession by Allah's permission on the Day, greatest of all the Prophet's ﷺ pleading for judgment to begin." },
    { term: "Salah", aliases: ["salat", "namaz"], def: "The prescribed prayer performed five times daily, the first deed weighed on the Day." },
    { term: "Qur'an", aliases: ["Quran", "the Book"], def: "The literal word of Allah, revealed to Muhammad ﷺ over twenty-three years and preserved exactly." }
  ]
};
window.NOOR_GUIDE = NOOR_GUIDE;
